'''Behavior''' refers to actions or patterns of activity of an organism or system in relation to internal and external conditions. In biology and psychology, examples include movement, communication, foraging, learning and deliberate inaction.

== Scope and definitions ==
Levitis, Lidicker and Freund (2009) documented substantial disagreement among behavioral biologists and proposed a definition focused on coordinated organism-level responses, distinguishing them from developmental changes. Definitions may differ for cellular activity, collective behavior and software-system behavior. Define the unit of analysis rather than assume all activity counts as behavior in the same sense.

== Mechanism and explanation ==
Behavior can be studied at several levels: proximate sensory and physiological mechanisms, developmental history, adaptive function and evolutionary history. An observed action does not by itself reveal its function or subjective motivation. A stimulus–response description may be sufficient for prediction in a controlled task but inadequate when internal state, learning or context changes.

== Experimental methods ==
Use an ethogram with explicit categories, observation intervals and criteria for ambiguous events. Record frequency, duration, latency or sequence as appropriate; account for sampling, observer bias and individual dependence. For example, compare foraging choices under randomized resource conditions and model repeated observations within animals. Separately measure physiological variables if attributing behavior to a mechanism. Computational agents can have operationally specified actions, but behavioral similarity alone is not evidence of biological or conscious equivalence.

